fter trailing for most of the middle game. The 14k's J6 on move 34 lost 15.9 points and flipped the lead from -9.4 to +6.6 for Black. A few moves later Opus 5.5's own J3 lost 42.9 points, but the 14k's G1 lost 43.0 and gave it all back. After that, Black captured 15 stones, the central White group was gone, and the rest was tidy endgame.", "moments": [{"turn": 15, "text": "Black's C6 lost 5.3 points and took the lead from -4.4 to -9.7. The engine's choice was B4, which Opus 5.5 only played two moves later. It was the biggest early slip, and Black stayed behind well into the middle game."}, {"turn": 34, "text": "The 14k's J6 lost 15.9 points and moved the lead from -9.4 to +6.6 for Black. The engine wanted H3 here. It was the first real crack in a game White had been leading."}, {"turn": 35, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with H3, and the analysis shows it gaining ground: the lead went from +6.6 to +17.9. The engine's own choice was G4, but H3 still pushed Black firmly into the lead."}, {"turn": 41, "text": "Black's J3 lost 42.9 points and turned a lead of +33.3 into -9.6. The engine's choice was J4, the point Opus 5.5 only filled on move 45. It was the one blunder of Opus 5.5's game."}, {"turn": 42, "text": "The 14k's G1 lost 43.0 points and put the lead back at +33.5, so the whole gift was returned in one move. The engine's choice was H1, which Black took on the next move. From here Black captured 15 stones and the game stayed above +27 to the end."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties, flagging those with two or fewer, and count.py flood-fills empty regions to tally stones and territory plus komi. Both only inspect the current position, and neither generates, scores, or ranks candidate moves or looks ahead.", "stop": false}}